; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED. port3's question on industrial hardware, one difficulty band up.
;
; WHY A SECOND TERMINATED BOARD AT ALL. port3 asks the whole task -- a run that
; starts at a fitting, crosses three stations on required sides, and terminates
; in a socket -- and it asks it at L1. Thirteen degrees of accumulated turning
; is a cable being nudged past three posts. What it cannot show is whether the
; policy has learned to weave or has learned this bench, because at L1 the
; difference does not cost anything.
;
; WHAT CHANGES. The stations. An F1 spool is laboratory hardware: a 4.75 mm
; shaft between two flanges, sitting loose on a bench. A cable cleat is what a
; harness is actually dressed against inside a machine -- bolted to a
; backplane, a 19 mm standoff with a retaining cap, five of them down a panel
; where the drilling allowed. It is a fixture with a catalogue number rather
; than one from a manipulation benchmark.
;
; THE LOOK AND THE DIFFICULTY ARE THE SAME CHANGE, which is the part worth
; stating. A crossing's turn is set by how far off the station's axis a seated
; cable rests -- the fixture's radius plus the cable's -- against the gap to the
; next station. A cleat's standoff is twice a spool's shaft, so the same cable
; on the same bench rests 13 mm off each axis instead of 8.25 and has to make a
; larger excursion at every station. Five stations on this spacing accumulate
; 142.9 degrees against port3's 12.6: L2, and mid-band rather than at an edge,
; so a board the sampler has yawed and jittered is still the level this document
; says it is. (The sampler checks that per draw rather than trusting it: over
; 200 draws of an earlier version, 47 of 200 episodes were not L2 at all.)
;
; IN LINE, and deliberately. `_scatter` exists because a row of thin posts at
; one x is the easiest thing there is to perceive and to plan for -- one
; standoff serves every station. That argument does not survive a fixture this
; thick: the required side of a 19 mm cleat is a real excursion, so the
; alternation carries the difficulty and the uneven gaps (88, 74, 84, 78 mm)
; carry the irregularity. An x stagger on top measured 178 degrees, hard against
; the L2 ceiling, where jitter would keep pushing the variant into L3.
;
; THE ENDS ARE port3'S, unchanged: the run is welded to an anchor bracket and
; must finish inside a port. Nothing about the question moves, so a score here
; and a score there differ in the board and not in what was asked.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem DRESS_THE_HARNESS_ACROSS_FIVE_CLEATS_INTO_THE_PORT)

  (:suite cable_route)

  (:language dress the cable through the five panel cleats and route its free end into the port)

  (:staging
    (:cameras cable_panel_l2@v1)
    ; Five crossings against three, on a longer run: the same per-crossing
    ; budget port3 gets, not a tighter one. A step limit that bit would make
    ; this a speed test wearing a difficulty label.
    (:max-steps 9000)
    ; port3's sampler: the panel is put down somewhere else every episode, the
    ; port follows it, the anchor bracket follows the cable, and the rod's own
    ; shape is drawn beside them.
    (:episodes (:count 30) (:seed 0) (:sampler staging:WEAVE_RIGID))
  )

  (:objects
    cable - panel_l2_cable
  )

  (:fixtures
    peg0   - panel_l2_peg0
    peg1   - panel_l2_peg1
    peg2   - panel_l2_peg2
    peg3   - panel_l2_peg3
    peg4   - panel_l2_peg4
    ; The station names stay `peg{i}` across both boards on purpose. Every goal
    ; clause, sampler and perception node addresses a station by that name;
    ; renaming the part would rename the question.
    anchor - panel_l2_anchor
    port   - panel_l2_port
  )

  ; Sides -1, +1, -1, +1, -1: the same alternation, two crossings longer.
  ;
  ; The seat boxes are port3's, unchanged, and they did not need changing. A box
  ; reaching 3 to 45 mm off the station's axis holds a cable seated at 8.25 mm
  ; against a spool and one seated at 13 mm against a cleat -- both are inside
  ; it, so the same tolerance scores both boards and the number says how well
  ; the route ran rather than which fixture it ran against.
  (:regions
    (seat0    (:target peg0) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.035 -0.003 0.035))))
    (seat1    (:target peg1) (:ranges (( 0.003 -0.035  0.045 0.035))))
    (seat2    (:target peg2) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.035 -0.003 0.035))))
    (seat3    (:target peg3) (:ranges (( 0.003 -0.035  0.045 0.035))))
    (seat4    (:target peg4) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.035 -0.003 0.035))))
    (portbox  (:target port) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.045  0.045 0.045))))
  )

  ; No init constraint: both arms at home, touching nothing. See weave4.
  (:init)

  ; Six clauses under one worst_of, so each crossing and the port are a sixth of
  ; the route each.
  ;
  ; THE FRACTIONS ARE SCALED TO THIS ROD, not copied. port3 asks 0.047 of a
  ; 0.82 m rod for a seat and 0.071 for the port -- three and five bodies of the
  ; 68 it carries. This rod is 0.90 m and the segment count scales with length,
  ; so it carries 76 and the same lengths of cable are 0.043 and 0.064 of it.
  ; Copying port3's numbers would have asked this board for MORE cable per
  ; crossing than port3 asks, and called the difference difficulty.
  (:goal
    (worst_of
      (And
        (>= (containment cable seat0) 0.043)
        (>= (containment cable seat1) 0.043)
        (>= (containment cable seat2) 0.043)
        (>= (containment cable seat3) 0.043)
        (>= (containment cable seat4) 0.043)
        (>= (containment cable portbox) 0.064)
      )
      (And
        (> (tcp-distance cable) 0.05)
        (<= (speed cable) 0.05)
      )
    )
  )

  (:observe
    (once (<= (tcp-distance cable) 0.05))
    (straightness cable)
  )

  ; The same graphs the weaves run; the port is one more station to them. See
  ; port2.
  (:artifacts
    (:routine "routine:RoutineWeave")
    (:graph gt "graphs/weave")
    (:graph vision "graphs/weave_vision")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier authored)
    (:source "AUTHORED -- no upstream. port3's terminated run, staged on five bolted
              cable cleats instead of three ManipulationNet F1 spools.")
    (:note "The fixture is drawn from primitives by EntityCableCleat (tasks/cable_route/
            assets/terminals.py) rather than imported as a mesh: a 28 x 40 mm bolted foot, a
            19 mm standoff and a retaining cap. The standoff is what a cable bears against and
            what the asset declares its `shaft` feature about, so a seat measured here reads
            identically to one measured against a spool -- which is exactly why seat_offset is
            a function of the variant rather than a constant, and why this board measures
            142.9 deg and mid-L2 instead of the 92.5 deg a spool-sized seat would have
            reported, barely over L2's own floor.
            Five cleats at x = 0.535 on gaps of 88, 74, 84 and 78 mm. The rod is 0.90 m at 76
            capsules; the whole route, anchor to five seats to the port mouth, measures 751 mm,
            so 149 mm is left over.")
  )
)
